# Claude Code

claudecode.nvim connects the Claude Code CLI to Neovim the same way the VS Code
extension does:

- Claude always knows which file you are in and what you have selected.
- When Claude wants to edit a file, the change opens as a diff in Neovim, and
  you accept or reject it there.
- The conversation itself runs in a panel on the right.

    `<Space>ac`{normal}   open / hide the Claude panel
    `<Space>af`{normal}   jump to the panel (opens it if needed)
    `<Space>ar`{normal}   resume an earlier conversation (pick from a list)
    `<Space>aC`{normal}   continue the most recent conversation
    `<Space>am`{normal}   choose the model

Start with a fresh playground, so Claude's edits land there:

    :Playground!

# Lesson 5.1: The panel

 1. In the playground tab, press `<Space>ac`{normal}. Claude starts in the playground
    folder. The first time, it asks whether to trust the folder.
 2. Ask: `What does shop.py do?` and press `<Enter>`{normal}.
 3. The panel is a terminal. Leave typing mode with `<C-q>`{normal} (not `<Esc><Esc>`{normal},
    which Claude uses itself). Now scroll, search and yank as in any buffer.
 4. `<C-h>`{normal} moves to the code window. `<Space>af`{normal} jumps back into Claude, ready
    to type.

# Lesson 5.2: Giving Claude context

Claude sees the current file and selection automatically. Look for "In
shop.py" or "N lines selected" under Claude's input box. To attach something
explicitly:

    `<Space>as`{normal}   (visual mode) send the selected lines
    `<Space>ab`{normal}   add the current file
    `<Space>as`{normal}   (in the file tree) add the file or folder under the cursor

Sending adds an `@file#L1-5` reference to Claude's input box. Keep typing
your question after it.

 1. In `inventory.py`, select the `restock` function with `V`{normal} and a motion,
    then press `<Space>as`{normal}.
 2. In the Claude panel, after the reference, type
    `Add input validation: amount must not be negative` and `<Enter>`{normal}.

# Lesson 5.3: Reviewing Claude's edits

When Claude proposes an edit, a diff opens: your file on one side, Claude's
version on the other. Claude waits until you decide.

    `<Space>aa`{normal}  or  `:w`{vim}   accept the change
    `<Space>ad`{normal}  or  `:q`{vim}   reject it (Claude is told, and you can say why)
    `]c`{normal} / `[c`{normal}               jump between changed blocks in the diff

You can edit Claude's side of the diff before accepting; your edits are kept.

 1. Wait for the diff from Lesson 5.2. Use `]c`{normal} to look through it.
 2. Change the error message on Claude's side to something of your own, then
    `<Space>aa`{normal}. Check `inventory.py`: it has your version.
 3. Ask Claude to `Rename Item to Product everywhere`. When the first diff
    opens, reject it with `<Space>ad`{normal} and tell Claude `Keep the name Item`.

# Lesson 5.4: Claude Code's own keys

Inside the panel, in typing mode, these are Claude Code's keys, not Neovim's:

    `<Esc>`{normal}          interrupt Claude
    `<Esc><Esc>`{normal}     go back to an earlier message and edit it
    `<S-Tab>`{normal}        cycle permission modes (ask before edits / accept edits / plan)
    `/`{normal}              slash commands, e.g. `/clear`, `/compact`, `/model`
    `@`{normal}              mention a file by name
    `!`{normal}              run a shell command

# Summary

    `<Space>ac`{normal} toggle   `<Space>af`{normal} focus    `<Space>ar`{normal} resume   `<Space>aC`{normal} continue
    `<Space>as`{normal} send selection / tree item   `<Space>ab`{normal} add current file
    `<Space>aa`{normal} accept diff   `<Space>ad`{normal} reject diff   `<C-q>`{normal} normal mode in the panel

